# Gateway for a breakpoint session. Caddy listens on :8080 and multiplexes a
# SINGLE tunnel hostname across several local services by path.
#
# WHY A GATEWAY AT ALL, AND WHY IT IS NOT A "VNC HELPER"
# A quick tunnel gives you exactly ONE hostname. Without path multiplexing you
# must choose: tunnel the application, or tunnel the desktop, never both. The
# catch-all rule at the bottom is the load-bearing one -- it is what makes this
# a gateway rather than a noVNC front end.
#
# Routing:
#   /health     -> answered HERE, by Caddy itself
#   /desktop    -> redirect into an already-connected noVNC session
#   /desktop/*  -> noVNC static assets      (websockify on :6080)
#   /vnc        -> the VNC WebSocket stream (websockify on :6080)
#   /*          -> the application          (nginx on :80)
#
# THE /health ROUTE IS NEW AND IS A BUG FIX. The deleted
# .ci/docker/desktop/Caddyfile had no /health rule, so /health fell through to
# the catch-all and hit nginx. Combined with the fact that the old workflow
# never actually started nginx, the tunnel's own health gate could not pass even
# in principle -- it burned three 60s attempts per matrix leg and `|| true`
# swallowed the failure. Answering /health at the gateway makes tunnel liveness
# independent of whether the app behind it is up, which is the correct split:
# transport liveness and application health are different questions.
#
# HOST NETWORKING IS LOAD-BEARING, not a convenience. The desktop stack (Xvfb,
# x11vnc, websockify) runs DIRECTLY ON THE RUNNER, not in a container, so
# `localhost:6080` only resolves for a container sharing the host's network
# namespace. Same for nginx on :80 when the compose stack is up.
#
# GROWING THIS: the hub product already routes code-server on 8080, ttyd on 7681
# and noVNC on 6080 over one hostname (see packages/www/src/content/docs/*/hub.md,
# `rediacc.hub.route.*`). Adding /code or /term here later is a three-line edit
# rather than a re-architecture, which is the whole argument for a gateway over
# a static origin.

:8080 {
	# Answered by the gateway itself, so tunnel liveness never depends on the
	# application being up. Must stay ABOVE the catch-all.
	handle /health {
		respond "breakpoint-origin-ok" 200
	}

	# Land on a session that is already connected, rather than on noVNC's
	# host/port prompt. `path=vnc` matches the /vnc WebSocket route below.
	redir /desktop /desktop/vnc.html?path=vnc&autoconnect=true permanent

	# The VNC WebSocket data channel -- the actual pixel stream.
	# websockify validates the Host header, so it is rewritten rather than
	# passed through as the tunnel hostname.
	handle /vnc {
		reverse_proxy localhost:6080 {
			header_up Host localhost:6080
			header_up X-Forwarded-For {remote_host}
		}
	}

	# noVNC's static assets. `handle_path` (not `handle`) strips the /desktop
	# prefix before proxying, because websockify serves the web root at /.
	handle_path /desktop/* {
		reverse_proxy localhost:6080
	}

	# Everything else is the application. THIS is what makes one tunnel serve
	# both the app and the desktop. If no app is running this returns 502,
	# which is correct and visible rather than silently empty.
	handle {
		reverse_proxy localhost:80
	}
}
